The buggy method is `org.apache.commons.lang.text.StrBuilder.contains(char)`.

Diagnosis:
- The failing test deletes `"three"` from `"onetwothree"`, leaving logical content `"onetwo"`.
- Then `contains('h')` should return `false`.
- But `contains(char)` is implemented as:

```java
public boolean contains(char ch) {
    char[] thisBuf = buffer;
    for (int i = 0; i < thisBuf.length; i++) {
        if (thisBuf[i] == ch) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
```

Root cause:
- It iterates over `thisBuf.length`, which is the backing array capacity, not the actual string length.
- `deleteFirst("three")` eventually calls `deleteImpl(...)`, which shifts characters and decrements `size`, but does not clear the trailing unused characters:

```java
private void deleteImpl(int startIndex, int endIndex, int len) {
    System.arraycopy(buffer, endIndex, buffer, startIndex, size - endIndex);
    size -= len;
}
```

So after deletion, stale characters from the old content remain beyond `size`.
`contains(char)` incorrectly scans those stale trailing characters and can find `'h'` from the deleted `"three"`.

Why this matches the failure:
- The assertion says `contains(char)` is “looking beyond the end of the string”.
- That is exactly what happens: it scans the full buffer instead of only indices `[0, size)`.

Dynamic verification was inconclusive due to debugger session loss, but the static evidence is strong and directly explains the observed failure.

